    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d798">

                <pb facs="0747" n="739" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg739"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102S/2062: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-06-04T11:00:00+09:00">June 4, 1940—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 2 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>404. Department’s 218, May 18, 4 p.m., and Shanghai’s 410, May 12, 1 p.m.,
                    terrorist activities. My British colleague<anchor n="82" xml:id="d798-f1"/> has
                    informed me that he is now prepared to make representations to the Foreign
                    Office. I propose to make an oral and informal approach to the Foreign Office as
                    suitable occasion offers within the next few days urging the advisability of
                    doing away with unlawful armed groups in areas under Japanese control at
                    Shanghai.</p>
                <p>Shanghai please repeat to Chungking and Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d798-f1" n="82" xml:id="d798-f1-content">
                <p>Sir Robert L. Craigie British Ambassador in Japan.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
